  "markdown_content": "## Current Outbreak Status\\nTexas is experiencing a concerning outbreak of measles with health officials reporting 561 confirmed cases across the state. The highly contagious virus spreads through respiratory droplets and can remain in the air for up to two hours after an infected person leaves an area.\\n\\n## Prevention\\nHealth experts emphasize the importance of vaccination as the most effective prevention method. The MMR vaccine provides 97% protection against measles when both recommended doses are received.\\n\\n## Symptoms and Guidelines\\nSymptoms typically appear 7-14 days after exposure and include:\\n* High fever\\n* Cough\\n* Runny nose\\n* Characteristic red, blotchy rash\\n\\nOfficials advise anyone experiencing these symptoms to isolate and contact their healthcare provider immediately.\\n\\n## Response Measures\\nThe Texas Department of Health Services is working closely with local authorities to contain the outbreak and has established emergency vaccination clinics in affected areas.",
